course: religion-ethics
lesson: 38
title: The Golden Rule in many traditions
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Golden Rule says: treat others as you would like to be treated. Some form of it is found in almost every religion and in humanism too.
explain: Jesus put it positively in Matthew 7:12: "Do to others what you would have them do to you", so it asks us to do good, not only to avoid harm.
explain: Jews read "Love your neighbour as yourself" in Leviticus 19:18, and Rabbi Hillel said "What is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbour: that is the whole Torah, the rest is commentary." Islam, Hinduism, Buddhism, Sikhism and Confucius teach the same idea in their own words.
explain: The rule needs wisdom: you may love surprise parties while your friend hates them, so the "platinum rule" says treat others as they would like to be treated.
explain: For a child today it is a meeting place: a Christian, a Muslim, a Hindu friend and a friend with no religion can all agree on it, and each can live it at school tomorrow.
fact: The Golden Rule says: treat others as you would like to be treated.
fact: Some form of the Golden Rule is found in almost every religious and non-religious tradition.
fact: In Judaism, Leviticus 19:18 says "Love your neighbour as yourself".
fact: Rabbi Hillel, a Jewish teacher who lived shortly before Jesus, taught: "What is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbour"; he called this the whole Torah and the rest commentary.
fact: Jesus said: "Do to others what you would have them do to you" (Matthew 7:12).
fact: Jesus' version is positive (do good); many others are negative (do not harm); the negative form is sometimes called the "silver rule".
fact: A saying of the Prophet Muhammad teaches: "None of you truly believes until he wishes for his brother what he wishes for himself".
fact: The Hindu Mahabharata says one should never do to another what one regards as hurtful to oneself.
fact: The Buddhist Udanavarga says: "Hurt not others in ways that you yourself would find hurtful".
fact: Sikh scripture teaches seeing all people as equal, and the ancient Chinese teacher Confucius said: "Do not impose on others what you do not wish for yourself".
fact: Humanists use the Golden Rule as a reasoned basis for treating others well, without needing belief in God.
fact: People want different things, so the "platinum rule" says: treat others as they would like to be treated.
exercise: What does the Golden Rule say? | Treat others as you would like to be treated | Treat others the way they have treated you before | Treat others well only if they are your friends | Treat others as your parents tell you to treat them
exercise: Why might so many traditions have found the same rule? | People everywhere can imagine how others feel | One teacher wrote it down and posted it worldwide | It was a law passed by every country together | It is a coincidence that has no reason at all
exercise: Which words come from the Jewish scriptures, Leviticus 19:18? | Love your neighbour as yourself | Do not impose on others what you dislike | Hurt not others in ways you find hurtful | Wish for your brother what you wish for you
exercise: What did Hillel mean by "the rest is commentary"? | The rest of the Torah explains how to live out this one rule | The rest of the Torah is not important and can be skipped | The rest of the Torah was written much later by other people | The rest of the Torah is only about the history of kings
exercise: Where did Jesus say "Do to others what you would have them do to you"? | Matthew 7:12 | Genesis 1:1 | Leviticus 19:18 | Psalm 23:1
exercise: What is the difference between the "do" and the "do not do" versions? | "Do" asks you to do good; "do not do" asks you to avoid harm | "Do" is for adults and "do not do" is for children only | "Do" is about God and "do not do" is about animals only | There is no difference at all; both ask for exactly the same thing from us
exercise: Which is the Islamic saying of the Prophet? | None of you truly believes until he wishes for his brother what he wishes for himself | Love your neighbour as yourself, for I am the Lord who made you both | Do to others what you would have them do to you, for this is the Law | Do not impose on others what you do not wish for yourself in your life
exercise: How is the Islamic saying similar to Jesus' words? | Both ask you to want and do good for others as for yourself | Both say you should only help people of your own religion | Both say you should wait until others are kind to you first | Both say you should never think about yourself at all
exercise: What is the shared idea in the Hindu and Jewish versions? | Do not do to others what would hurt or be hateful to you | Do whatever others ask you to do, whatever it is | Treat only your family the way you want to be treated | Do good only on holy days and festivals each year
exercise: Why does the Buddhist version focus on not hurting? | It teaches compassion for others' pain | Buddhism teaches that helping others is wrong | Buddhists believe nobody can ever be hurt at all | Buddhists only care about animals, not people
exercise: Which saying comes from Confucius? | Do not impose on others what you do not wish for yourself | Love your neighbour as yourself, for this is the Law | Hurt not others in ways that you yourself find hurtful | Do to others what you would have them do to you
exercise: Why is the negative form sometimes called the "silver rule"? | It tells you what not to do, not what good to do | It was first written down on a piece of silver | It is worth less than gold, so nobody should use it | It is only used in countries that have silver mines
exercise: Can someone follow the Golden Rule without believing in God? | Yes, humanists use it as a reasoned basis for kindness | No, the rule only works if you go to church every week | No, only religious people can feel what others feel | Yes, but only if they pretend to believe in God too
exercise: Your friend hates surprise parties but you love them. What does wise use of the Golden Rule say? | Treat her as she would like: no surprise party for her | Throw her one, because you would love one yourself | Throw her one, but invite only your own friends to it | Do nothing for her birthday, since rules are confusing
exercise: What does the "platinum rule" say? | Treat others as they would like to be treated | Treat others better than you treat yourself | Treat others only as well as they deserve | Treat others the way that a king treats them
test: My brother says the Golden Rule is only in the Bible. What would you tell him? | It is in almost every tradition, like Judaism, Islam, Hinduism and Buddhism | Yes, no other religion or way of life has ever taught anything like it | It was in the Bible first, and other religions later copied it from there | It is only in old books from China and has nothing to do with Christians
test: Priya, who is Hindu, asks: "Does your religion have a rule like ours about not hurting others?" What is the best answer? | Yes, Jesus taught "Do to others what you would have them do to you" | No, Christians do not have any rules about how to treat other people | Yes, but ours is the only true one, so yours does not really count | No, only Hindus think that hurting other people is a wrong thing
test: lol he pushed me first so i'm allowed to push him back, that's the golden rule innit | No, the rule is how you'd like to be treated, not paying back what you got | Yes, the Golden Rule means you do to others exactly what they did to you first | Yes, but only if a teacher saw him push you before you did it | No, the Golden Rule only applies to grown-ups, not to children
test: Your gran loves loud singing on her birthday; your shy friend would die of embarrassment. Using the platinum rule, what do you do for your friend? | A quiet card and a small gift, as she would like | Loud singing in front of the whole class, as gran likes | Loud singing, because the Golden Rule says do what you like | Nothing, because people differ and so rules never help
test: Tom, who has no religion, says, "I don't need God to know not to be mean." How does this fit the lesson? | Humanists also use the Golden Rule, by reason and empathy | It does not fit, because only believers can be kind at all | He is wrong, because the rule came from one church only | It fits, but only if Tom goes to Sunday school with you
test: Which version is the "silver rule"? | What is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbour | Do to others what you would have them do to you, as Jesus said | Treat others as they would like to be treated | Love your neighbour as you love your own self
test: My cousin says Hillel was a Christian saint. Put him right. | Hillel was a Jewish rabbi who taught before Jesus' ministry | Hillel was a Buddhist monk who lived in ancient India | Hillel was a Muslim scholar who wrote down the Qur'an | Hillel was a Roman soldier who guarded the Temple in Jerusalem
test: A judge asks: "If the Golden Rule is everywhere, why do people still do wrong?" What is the best reply? | Knowing a rule is not the same as living it; it takes practice | Because the rule is wrong, so people are right to ignore it most of the time | Because only some countries were ever told about the Golden Rule at all | Because the rule is only meant for festivals and holidays, not normal days
test: What would happen in a class where everyone followed the Golden Rule? | People would think how others feel before acting | Nobody would ever disagree about anything at all | Everyone would have to like the very same things | The teacher would no longer be needed at all
test: Which is true about the Golden Rule in Islam? | A saying of the Prophet links true belief with wishing good for others | Islam teaches that it is fine to hurt or cheat people who are not Muslims | Islam has no teaching at all about how to treat other people | The Golden Rule in Islam only applies to the oldest brother
test: shut up about rules, ur just being nice so people like u | Kindness is right even if no one notices; the rule is about others, not praise | That is true; the only reason for kindness is getting people to like you | That is true, so it is fine to stop being kind when nobody is watching | That is rude, so the Golden Rule says you can now be rude back to them
test: Harpreet, who is Sikh, asks if her faith has a Golden Rule too. What is the best answer? | Yes, Sikh scripture teaches seeing all people as equal | No, the Golden Rule is found only in the Bible and nowhere else | No, Sikhs have no teaching at all about how to treat others | Yes, but in Sikhism it only applies to other Sikhs, not to you
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Golden Rule? | That one ought to treat others as one would wish to be treated | That one ought to repay others in the manner of their conduct | That one ought to treat kindly one's friends exclusively | That one ought to obey without question all persons in authority
exam: Identify the source wherein Jesus is recorded as saying "Do to others what you would have them do to you". | The Gospel according to Matthew, chapter seven | The Book of Leviticus, chapter nineteen | The Mahabharata of the Hindu tradition | The Analects attributed to Confucius
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the distribution of the Golden Rule. | It is found in some form in almost every religious and non-religious tradition | It is found solely in the holy scriptures of the Christian tradition and in no other | It is found solely in traditions originating in Asia | It is found in no tradition prior to the modern period
exam: Pursuant to the "platinum rule", ascertain the proper conduct towards a person who dislikes surprises. | To treat that person as he or she would wish, refraining from surprises | To arrange a surprise regardless, given that one would oneself greatly enjoy one | To arrange a surprise, inviting solely one's own acquaintances | To abstain from any celebration whatsoever of that person
